  "response": "Adjudication begins by fixing what requires explanation, and the chronology here is unusually tight: the collapse of the eastern Mediterranean Bronze Age state system occurred within a few decades around 1200 BCE. That the Hittite capital Hattusa was burned and abandoned around 1200 BCE demonstrates how compressed that horizon was. Destruction layers of this kind furnish the fixed stratigraphic anchors against which every proposed mechanism must be dated, and a candidate cause that cannot be placed inside the window is disqualified before its explanatory merits are weighed at all.\n\nOne control on the evidence is the recognition that the documentary record is itself a product of the events it describes, so that what survives is never a neutral sample of what once existed. The Mycenaean palace centers at Pylos, Mycenae and Tiryns were destroyed by fire; as a result, the fires that destroyed the palace at Pylos baked and thereby preserved its Linear B tablets. Archives recovered under such conditions cluster at moments of crisis, and historians must correct for that bias before treating palace documents as representative of Late Bronze Age administration as a whole.\n\nLinear B was deciphered by Arthur Evans in 1900 during his excavations at Knossos. The standard procedure where texts can be read is to weigh their testimony against environmental proxies recovered independently of them. Tablets from the archives of Ugarit record urgent requests for grain shipments to relieve famine. Pollen cores from the Sea of Galilee indicate a prolonged dry period in the Levant between roughly 1250 and 1100 BCE. The Ugaritic appeals show, and the pollen sequence independently confirms, that a severe multi-decade drought affected the eastern Mediterranean in the decades around 1200 BCE. Those appeals for grain were dispatched before armed attacks destroyed major palace centers of the eastern Mediterranean in the decades around 1200 BCE. What the combined record will bear is that at least one of the two \u2014 the drought or the armed destructions \u2014 was at work in the fall of the palaces, and perhaps both were.\n\nRival reconstructions are tested against absolute dating and against the record of what followed the destructions. The eruption of Thera in 1180 BCE buried the palaces of Crete and set off the regional collapse. A review of Aegean absolute chronology concluded that the Thera eruption predates the Late Bronze Age collapse by roughly four centuries, a result that cannot be squared with an eruption that levelled the palaces at the very moment of their fall. Either Ugarit was resettled as a major urban center within a century of its destruction, or it was never reoccupied as a major urban center after its final destruction; one of those two must be the case, and they cannot both hold. No single cause by itself accounts for the end of the Late Bronze Age palace system, a verdict that cannot be true alongside the attribution of the whole collapse to the Theran eruption, and the discipline's working method is accordingly to specify how drought, warfare and the failure of exchange networks reinforced one another.",
